<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest. Every app uploaded to App Store Connect needs one, and the
  aggregate of this file and every bundled framework's own is what Apple checks.
  Confirm the aggregate with Product > Archive > Generate Privacy Report before
  an upload rather than trusting this file alone.

  All four answers are the empty one, and each is a claim about the binary:

  - NSPrivacyTracking false, NSPrivacyTrackingDomains empty: the app contacts
    nothing. package.mjs fails the build on any asset fetched over http(s), and
    it replaces ScoreClient().auto(MC_SCORE_OPTS) with an unconnected client, so
    there is no network code left to track anybody with.
  - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes empty: nothing is collected. What the game keeps
    stays in localStorage on the device.
  - NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes empty. The one most guides get wrong is
    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults, which is required when *your*
    binary or a bundled SDK calls NSUserDefaults. localStorage in a WKWebView is
    WebKit's own store and WebKit is a system framework; @capacitor/ios contains
    no UserDefaults call and ships its own manifest declaring none. This app
    bundles no other plugin at all, not even @capacitor/haptics, so there is
    nothing else to aggregate. Adding @capacitor/preferences, or anything that
    writes UserDefaults from Swift, changes that, and so does adding file
    timestamp, disk space or system boot time APIs.

  Unlike george-boole there is **no human-readable privacy page yet**.
  App Store Connect requires one per app, so magmacrunch.com/privacy/makemecookies/
  has to exist before submission, and once it does the two must stay in step.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
